sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design, ADR 0260 build step 4 (docs/reference/development-containers.md#the-seed-on-each-ci-node): each Node that offers proof places, other than the default's own Node, keeps its own seed of the proof Project's default. One run per seed of the default sends the seed to every CI Node whose seed is behind; each target may fail alone.
  %% Decided by the main orchestrator (2026-11-16): Incus moves the seed, `incus copy --refresh --mode=push` from the default's Node to each CI Node over WireGuard. Push mode needs a listener on the target only, so the default's Node gets a client remote and no daemon change. Each CI Node listens on its WireGuard address and trusts the default Node's client certificate for `orbit-instances-nested` only (docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#the-seed-listener). `--refresh` sends only the snapshots that changed. Rejected: one seed that every copy fetches over the network at its start (ADR 0260), a seed Instance per CI Node that refreshes its own fleet, and a ZFS stream through the Swamp worker with bookmarks of Orbit's own.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: none · after the proof default records a seed, or a CI Node's ci-runner add, role converge or node converge passes
  %% kind: instance.seed_copy
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane C icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant N as Default Node<br/>holds the default
  participant C as CI Node<br/>each target
  Note over GW: Admit the seed copy<br/>CI Nodes behind the seed
  %%> Started by:
  %%> - the proof default records a new seed: the refresh after a release, or an `instance:deploy` of the default
  %%> - a `ci-runner` add or role converge of a Node that offers proof places passes, or a `node:converge` of such a Node whose roles phase passed: a Node behind the seed catches up after any passed converge
  %%> - a seed copy ends, only when the default's seed moved during it (all Nodes behind the new seed), or for Nodes that no seed copy has tried at the same seed (a Node that became a target while the copy held the lock, or one that the run's target limit left out)
  %%> Never started by:
  %%> - the end of a copy for a Node that any seed copy has tried at the same seed: it waits for the next seed or for a passed converge of a CI Node (that admission takes every Node behind the seed), so lasting failures do not repeat, also not two Nodes for each other
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the proof Project's default is active and has a seed; a seed is its commit and the time the default took it
  %%> - targets: every Node that offers proof places (`ci-runner`, CI places, with or without `app-dev`), other than the default's Node, whose recorded seed is not that seed (docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#proof-copies-on-a-ci-node), and whose seed listener is ready: its `nodes.seed_sender_id` names the default's Node; with none, nothing is admitted
  %%> - every run that changes the listener records `seed_sender_id` by one rule: a `ci-runner` add, role converge or removal, and a `node:converge` with the role; a passed add or role converge sets the sender, a `node:converge` whose roles phase passed keeps only the sender it already names, and any other end clears it (docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#the-seed-listener)
  %%> - when the default moves to another Node, no CI Node is a target until its `ci-runner` role is converged again
  %%> Not a target:
  %%> - a Node whose listener is not ready: no copy fails on it and no copy marks it tried; its next passed `ci-runner` converge makes it a target
  %%> - one copy at a time: an open copy holds the default's seed hold, `operation.target_busy`
  %%> - while a deployment of the default holds the seed or waits for it: the admission is refused, `instance.seed_held`, and the deployment's end admits it again (the seed moved or the Nodes are still behind); so a copy that ends while a deployment waits admits no next copy, and the deployment gets the hold next
  %%> The seed hold (one rule for one resource, docs/reference/development-containers.md#the-seed-hold):
  %%> - one hold key per default, `instance_seed:DEFAULT_ID`: the seed copy takes it as its Operation lock at admission and keeps it to its end, so every push of the run sends a seed that no retake deletes or renames
  %%> - a deployment of the default asks the Gateway for the seed before its first `seed` phase and keeps it to its end; it waits while a seed copy is open (see container/deploy-default.mmd)
  %%> - a Node past the run's target limit: the copies share the default Node's uplink, so the run budget grows with the targets and must fit in the Swamp serve run cap; the limit is the number of copy steps that fit beside the fixed phases (`ProofSeeds::maxTargets`), and the end of the run admits the Nodes it left out; a `ci-runner` add or converge names its own Node, and the limit keeps that Node, because the end of a run admits no Node that a copy tried at the seed
  %%> Result:
  %%> - a task is recorded with the full intent: the default's Node, container and inner volume, per target its Node and WireGuard address, and `release_base` when the run's targets are all the Nodes behind the seed
  %%> - the run budget is captured: the fixed phases plus one copy step bound per target (`ProofSeeds::budgetSeconds`)
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>instance-seed-copy
  loop each CI Node, side by side
    SW->>C: Read its Incus certificate<br/>Ansible · tags certificate
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - reads the Incus server certificate of the CI Node and checks that the Node listens on its WireGuard address
    %%> On failure:
    %%> - no listener: this target fails; a `ci-runner` converge of the Node adds the listener and the trust
    %%> - the Node is unreachable: this target fails with the SSH error as its detail, in this phase and in each later one; the copy and the check do nothing for a target that failed
    %%> - the Node goes away in the middle of this phase: the phase leaves no certificate and no result, so the copy step fails the target as `The certificate phase ended without a result` and does nothing else for it; a copy that ends the same way leaves no `copied-ID` mark, so the check step fails the target as `The copy phase ended without a result` and checks no holder, which may hold a seed of the same name from an earlier copy
  end
  loop each CI Node, side by side
    SW->>N: Copy the seed<br/>Ansible · tags copy
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - adds the client remote `proof-node-ID` of root when it is missing, one copy at a time under a lock file, because root's client rewrites one file for all its remotes without a lock and two adds side by side would lose one, and refuses when the certificate that root's client stored for it is not the one read on the CI Node
    %%> - names the base: when the default holds a `base-COMMIT` pair and the holder holds `seed-COMMIT`, renames the holder's pair to `base-COMMIT` through the remote; a rename keeps the creation time, so both sides share that snapshot
    %%> - `incus storage volume copy` of the inner volume to `proof-seed-inner` with `--refresh --mode=push`, then `incus copy DEFAULT proof-node-ID:proof-seed` with the same flags, in `orbit-instances-nested`
    %%> Why push:
    %%> - the source server sends the data and only the target listens, so the default's Node needs no listener and no daemon change
    %%> Why refresh:
    %%> - Incus compares the snapshots on both sides by name and creation time and sends only the newer ones, from the newest shared one; the holder gets `seed-COMMIT` natively; the first copy to a Node sends the whole pair
    %%> Why a base:
    %%> - a retake deletes or renames the old `seed-COMMIT`, so without the default's `base-COMMIT` no snapshot is shared and the refresh sends the whole pair; the retake keeps it while a ready CI Node records that seed (`ProofSeeds`)
    %%> Why the whole instance:
    %%> - a snapshot source carries no snapshots, so a refresh from it could not find a common one
    %%> Why side by side:
    %%> - one push of an incremental seed takes about 30 s; one after the other, the run took the sum, and a slow target delayed the next
    %%> - each push of the default's mounted inner volume freezes its file system (`fsfreeze`) and thaws it at once, before it sends anything; a freeze while the other push holds its own fails with `Failed freezing filesystem`, so the copy tries only that push again, up to five times, 2 s apart
    %%> On failure:
    %%> - this target fails with `instance.seed_copy_failed`; the other targets' copies still run
  end
  loop each CI Node, side by side
    SW->>C: Check the holder<br/>Ansible · tags check
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the holder `proof-seed` has the snapshot `seed-COMMIT` that the run was admitted for, and its volume has the snapshot of the same name
    %%> - the holder's config and devices match the default's, as the start guard checks them; the volume has exactly the inner volume's keys
  end
  opt every Node behind the seed was a target and passed
    SW->>N: Release the base<br/>Ansible · tags release
    %%> When:
    %%> - the intent's `release_base`: the run's targets are all the Nodes behind the default's seed (`ProofSeeds`); every target passed; the default still holds a snapshot with the name the run was admitted for (by name only: after a retake at the same commit during the run, the base that the retake kept goes too)
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - deletes every `base-` pair of the default; the holders drop it at their next refresh
    %%> On failure:
    %%> - no target's result changes; the next retake deletes the base
  end
  SW-->>GW: Run ended<br/>result per target
  %%> How the Gateway learns it:
  %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
  Note over GW: Record each new seed
  %%> Result:
  %%> - each Node whose target passed records the seed it was admitted for: its commit and its time; it offers proof places again
  %%> - a proof copy on that Node clones `proof-seed/seed-COMMIT` with the clone step of every copy
  opt a target failed
    Note over GW: Keep the old seed<br/>Activity per failed Node
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the failed Node keeps its recorded seed and offers no proof places until a copy passes; copies made earlier keep running
    %%> - the Activity row `instance.seed_copy_failed` names the Node, the Operation and the detail
    %%> - the release and the default are unchanged; the next seed or a passed converge of a CI Node copies again, and a rerun is safe
  end
  opt seed moved, or new targets
    Note over GW: Admit the next copy<br/>Nodes not at the seed
    %%> When:
    %%> - the default took a newer seed while the copy ran: every Node behind it is a target
    %%> - the seed is the same, and a Node that no seed copy has tried at this seed is behind it: only such Nodes are targets; the Gateway reads which Nodes were tried from the targets of the recorded `instance.seed_copy` Operations of that seed
    %%> Not:
    %%> - while a deployment of the default waits for the seed hold or holds it (`instance.seed_held`): the deployment goes first, and its end admits the copy
    %%> - for a Node that failed at the same seed
  end
